Hi Håvard and team.

In Section 1.3 in the INLA paper <https://rss.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2008.00700.x> the authors define a LGM. They write the density of the latent field x conditional to a hyperparameter vector Theta1, and the density of y conditional to a hyperparameter vector Theta2. Later they join Theta1 and Theta2 into one unique hyperparameter vector Theta.

I'm new to the topic and I'm not sure I understand correctly the logic behind this formulation. Could you give an example of a hyperparameter that could go into Theta2?

Thank you

Guillermina

From u66ed4f72a898a51c at groups.google.com  Thu Sep 16 17:28:46 2021
From: u66ed4f72a898a51c at groups.google.com (Elias T. Krainski)
Date: Thu, 16 Sep 2021 17:28:46 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] Question about Rue et al. 2009 INLA paper
Message-ID: <YtozCliM7Js.3o9h_neECwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <YtozCliM7Js.rmejCq2CCwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <YtozCliM7Js.rmejCq2CCwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/YtozCliM7Js/m/3o9h_neECwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Example,
  y_i | x_i ~ N(x_i, a^2)
x_i ~ N(x_{i-1}, b^2)
so, x depends on b and y conditional on x depends on a, where a and b are hyperparameters. Generally speaking, one is the parameters of x (the latent field parameters -> hyperparameters) and the other of y conditional on y (the likelihood hyperparameters).

