Computing the inla-equivalent of the estimator you used on the brms output,

  hyp <- inla.hyperpar.sample(n = 1000, result = m_inla)
  phy_inla_like_brm = mean(hyp[,2]^-0.5)^2 / sum(colMeans(hyp^-0.5)^2)

does bring the values closer together in some of the simulations, but it seems the precision/std.dev <http://std.dev>. estimates are generally different, and I would start by looking at the priors.

Finn

